          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Peer Gynt Suites</strong><br>\nOne of Grieg's most notable contributions to music was his work for Henrik Ibsen's play \"Peer Gynt.\" Grieg composed the incidental music for the play's premiere in 1876. The music he wrote for \"Peer Gynt\" includes some of his most well-known pieces, such as \"Morning Mood\" and \"In the Hall of the Mountain King.\" These pieces later became part of the two \"Peer Gynt\" suites.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence and Style</strong><br>\nGrieg was known for his ability to blend the piano with other instruments in unique ways, showcasing the instrument's capabilities. His style evolved throughout his career, but he maintained a distinctive Nordic sound. His music often mirrored the complexity of the Norwegian landscape, with dramatic shifts and a sense of narrative.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Struggles and Health</strong><br>\nThroughout his life, Grieg faced personal challenges, including health issues and the loss of his only child. Despite these difficulties, he continued to compose and perform. His health issues, particularly his lung condition, persisted and affected his productivity. However, Grieg's resilience allowed him to remain an influential figure in music until his later years.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
